# Apache config for pdoom1.com on DreamHost shared hosting.
#
# The load-bearing goal here is SURVIVING A TRAFFIC SPIKE. Shared hosting has a
# hard bandwidth budget and a modest CPU share; a sudden crowd (a reviewer post,
# a front-page link) is exactly when this file earns its keep. Compression cuts
# response size, caching cuts repeat origin hits, and dropping dead weight from
# the deploy cuts what has to be served at all (see the rsync excludes in
# .github/workflows/auto-deploy-on-push.yml).

RewriteEngine On

# Canonical domain: redirect www -> apex.
RewriteCond %{HTTP_HOST} ^www\.pdoom1\.com$ [NC]
RewriteRule ^(.*)$ https://pdoom1.com/$1 [R=301,L]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Retired URLs.
#
# /changelog/ -> /game-changelog/ (issue #141, Pip's call 2026-07-28).
# The site grew three changelog-ish URLs. /game-changelog/ is the live one (it
# is what navigation.js links as "Updates"); /changelog/ was orphaned, and the
# Airtable pipeline that fed its data has never once run successfully. It stays
# on disk rather than being deleted -- deploy is `rsync --delete`, so removing it
# from public/ would remove it from production and this is meant to be reversible.
#
# 301, not 302: the move is permanent, and the game's in-game "What's New"
# fallback now tells players "Visit pdoom1.com for the latest updates" -- so
# /changelog is the URL a person guesses. A 301 lets crawlers and browsers retire
# the old URL for good; a 302 would ask them to keep checking a dead page forever.
#
# The `^` anchor is what stops this looping: the target `game-changelog/` cannot
# match this pattern. Covered forms: /changelog, /changelog/, /changelog/index.html
# (a request for /changelog gets mod_dir's DirectorySlash 301 first, then this one).
#
# public/changelog/index.html ALSO carries a meta-refresh to the same target, as
# a fallback in case this directive does not take effect on the shared host.
# That page is not the mechanism -- this is -- but it must not be a dead end.
RewriteRule ^changelog(/|/index\.html)?$ /game-changelog/ [R=301,L]

# /docs/steam-readiness.md -> /docs/roadmap/ (issue #17, Pip's ruling 2026-07-31,
# landed 2026-08-06).
#
# That file was a 2025-09 build-checklist that had rotted into a straight
# falsehood. It said "This site now links to Steam for downloads" and carried
# three green ticks -- "Download CTAs: updated to prioritize Steam", "Steam
# badges: implemented", "Steam store URL added to config.json". None of those
# are true: config.json's `steamStoreUrl` is the empty string, the homepage Steam
# badge is commented out behind a `#TODO-real-steam-appid` placeholder, and
# p(Doom)1 has no store page. It returned HTTP 200 on production for nine days
# after the ruling. A funder or journalist reading it concludes the game is on
# Steam.
#
# Deleted rather than rewritten, because Pip's ruling is that Steam is off the
# public site until he is comfortable integrating -- an honest Steam page is
# still a Steam page. The truthful statement of where Steam sits lives on the
# roadmap ("Beta -- a Steam 'coming soon' page"), which is the redirect target.
#
# Redirected rather than left to 404, because the URL was linked from /docs/ and
# from the site nav for ~11 months, so it is a URL a person or a crawler holds.
# Same reasoning as /changelog/ above. The escaped dot matters: an unescaped `.`
# would also match /docs/steam-readinessXmd.
RewriteRule ^docs/steam-readiness\.md$ /docs/roadmap/ [R=301,L]

# /stats/ -> /game-stats/ (Pip's call 2026-08-06; closes TECH_DEBT A8's remainder).
#
# THIS RULE IS UNVERIFIABLE BEFORE MERGE AND MAY NEVER FIRE. DreamHost's shared
# hosting owns /stats/ itself: the path answers `401 Unauthorized` with
# `WWW-Authenticate: Basic realm="Statistics Area"`, the panel's own web-stats
# area, and that is configured above the document root. No visitor has ever been
# able to load anything we published there. Measured 2026-08-06:
#   curl -sI https://pdoom1.com/stats/                -> 401, realm "Statistics Area"
#   curl -sI https://pdoom1.com/stats/competition.html -> 401, same realm
#   curl -sI https://pdoom1.com/game-stats/            -> 200
#
# Evidence that the host wins: public/stats/.htaccess held an `AuthType None` /
# `Satisfy Any` override written specifically to defeat that prompt. It deployed
# and the 401 above is what it achieved. A docroot RewriteRule is a weaker lever
# than a directory-level auth override, so expect this to lose too.
#
# It is here anyway because the downside is nil -- the path already fails closed,
# so a rule that never fires leaves things exactly as they are -- and because if
# DreamHost's stats area is ever disabled for this domain, the URL lands on the
# page it should have been all along instead of a 404.
#
# VERIFY AFTER MERGE, NOT ON THE NETLIFY PREVIEW (Netlify ignores .htaccess):
#   curl -sI https://pdoom1.com/stats/     # 301 -> /game-stats/ = it took
#                                          # 401 (realm "Statistics Area") = host won
#
# The `^` anchor keeps the target /game-stats/ from matching, so this cannot
# loop. It also, deliberately, does not match /stats/competition.html: that page
# was NOT a duplicate of /game-stats/ and has no successor, so redirecting it
# would promise competition data and deliver game stats. See the commit message.
RewriteRule ^stats(/|/index\.html)?$ /game-stats/ [R=301,L]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compression -- the single biggest spike lever.
# events.json is ~1.18 MB uncompressed and is fetched on every /events/ visit;
# gzip takes it to ~200 KB. HTML/CSS/JS/SVG compress 70-80%.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
<IfModule mod_deflate.c>
  AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE text/html text/plain text/css text/xml
  AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE application/javascript application/x-javascript text/javascript
  AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE application/json
  AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE application/rss+xml application/atom+xml application/xml
  AddOutputFilterByType DEFLATE image/svg+xml
  # Proxies must not hand a compressed body to a client that can't take it.
  Header append Vary Accept-Encoding
</IfModule>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Caching. There is NO content hashing in filenames here (site.css stays
# site.css across deploys), so cache lifetimes trade spike relief against update
# latency. Images are content-stable and are the bandwidth hogs, so they get
# long lives; text that a deploy changes gets short ones.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
<IfModule mod_expires.c>
  ExpiresActive On

  # Images / fonts: rarely change, biggest bytes. Long cache = the largest
  # bandwidth saving under load (the 3.8 MB cat, the 2.6 MB logo, hero art).
  ExpiresByType image/png                 "access plus 30 days"
  ExpiresByType image/jpeg                "access plus 30 days"
  ExpiresByType image/webp                "access plus 30 days"
  ExpiresByType image/gif                 "access plus 30 days"
  ExpiresByType image/svg+xml             "access plus 7 days"
  ExpiresByType image/x-icon              "access plus 30 days"
  ExpiresByType font/woff2                "access plus 30 days"

  # CSS/JS: unfingerprinted, so a deploy's change must propagate. One hour
  # bounds staleness while still absorbing a spike's repeat loads.
  ExpiresByType text/css                  "access plus 1 hour"
  ExpiresByType application/javascript    "access plus 1 hour"
  ExpiresByType text/javascript           "access plus 1 hour"

  # Data a workflow refreshes (version.json, events.json, leaderboard). Kept
  # short so the homepage never shows a stale version; compression, not caching,
  # protects these under load.
  ExpiresByType application/json          "access plus 5 minutes"

  # Feeds: subscribers should see new posts promptly.
  ExpiresByType application/rss+xml       "access plus 30 minutes"
  ExpiresByType application/atom+xml      "access plus 30 minutes"

  # HTML: 10 minutes. Long enough to blunt a spike, short enough that a fix
  # pushed mid-event reaches people within the event.
  ExpiresByType text/html                 "access plus 10 minutes"

  # Markdown routes are re-typed to text/plain just below, and the roadmap is
  # edited often. Without this they inherited DreamHost's default (measured
  # 2026-07-28: `Cache-Control: max-age=172800`, i.e. two days).
  ExpiresByType text/plain                "access plus 10 minutes"
</IfModule>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Markdown routes -- TECH_DEBT B8.
#
# DreamHost's server-wide mime map types `.md` as `text/markdown`. Measured
# 2026-07-28: `curl -I https://pdoom1.com/docs/roadmap.md` returns
# `Content-Type: text/markdown`. No browser RENDERS that type, so it becomes a
# download: clicking "Roadmap" in the site nav saved a file instead of showing
# a page. Five routes are reachable this way and all are in the sitemap:
#   /docs/DEV_NOTES.md  /docs/how-leaderboards-work.md
#   /docs/pdoom1-open-issues.md  /docs/roadmap.md
# (/docs/steam-readiness.md was the fifth; it is deleted and 301'd above.)
#
# Re-typing to text/plain makes every one of them render in the browser. This
# is a floor, not the destination -- a reader gets raw markdown source, with
# `##` and pipe tables visible. The proper fix is an HTML surface that renders
# the markdown.
#
# CORRECTED 2026-08-06: this comment used to justify not building that surface
# by claiming /blog/post.html's parser "handles only links, images, inline code,
# bold and italic -- no headings, no tables, no fenced code". That was false when
# written and is false now. The parser has handled headings, fenced code, lists,
# blockquotes and hr since cf38e315, and GitHub-style pipe tables since
# 2026-08-03; `scripts/test-blog-render.js` asserts each one. The same wrong
# sentence lived in CLAUDE.md and was corrected there on 2026-08-03 -- this copy
# was missed. So reusing that renderer for /docs/*.md is now a live option, not a
# blocked one. It is still separate work and is deliberately not invented here.
#
# Content-type is chosen at the SERVER, so this fixes the routes without
# touching a single .md file (public/docs/roadmap.md's content is owned
# elsewhere right now) and without forking the content into an HTML copy.
#
# text/plain is already in the DEFLATE list above, so these still compress.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
<IfModule mod_mime.c>
  AddType text/plain .md
  AddCharset UTF-8 .md
</IfModule>

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Security hardening.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deny dotfiles (.htaccess, .git leftovers, etc.)
<FilesMatch "^\.">
  Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

# No directory listings.
Options -Indexes

<IfModule mod_headers.c>
  Header set X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
  Header set X-Frame-Options "SAMEORIGIN"
  Header set Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
</IfModule>
